* atexit callback registry. Callbacks have the C ABI and take no arguments.
*
* 2026-09-23 (found while investigating a fresh-build qclib crash unrelated
* to long long): the original version put ax_count/ax_index/ax_table in a
* separate "vsect" block and referenced them as BARE absolute labels
* ("move.l ax_count,d0"). Live reproduction: even a program that never
* calls atexit() crashed with a PMMU fault inside tc_run_atexit (called by
* every exit()), jumping through a garbage ax_table entry. A byte dump at
* the faulting instruction showed the exact absolute address ql68k had
* resolved "ax_count" to: $FFFF82D0 -- nowhere near this tiny module's
* actual storage, clearly a wrong bare-absolute-vsect-reference relocation.
* A first attempt to fix this by keeping the vsect but switching to
* PC-relative addressing ("ax_count(pc)") does not even assemble: qr68k
* rejects it outright ("PC-Bezug auf einen anderen Abschnitt") because a
* vsect is a genuinely separate section from the surrounding psect, unlike
* what it looks like from the source layout.
* Real fix: drop the vsect entirely and declare the three fields as plain
* data directly inside this file's OWN psect, exactly the convention QCC's
* own generated code already uses successfully for its module-local
* statics (see tc_io_buf/tc_io_cnt in qcc_backend_c.cpp's emitM68kCore --
* plain "dc.l" lines inside the psect, no vsect, verified on real 68030
* hardware across the whole C89 test history). Referenced via PC-relative
* addressing, now valid since they live in the SAME section as the code
* that reads them. This intentionally does NOT touch q9_cstart.a's own
* vsect (that one is legitimately cross-module, per-process OS-9 storage
* reached via the Microware a6-relative convention) or the several other
* qclib leaf modules (rand/getc/perror/putc/locale/strerror/round) that
* still use vsect+bare-label for their own local statics: none of those
* currently use their stored value as a jump-table index, so the same
* underlying relocation defect stays latent there rather than crashing --
* worth an audit of its own, out of scope for this fix.
* tc_run_atexit reloads every "(pc)" pointer fresh after each callback
* invocation: an atexit() callback is arbitrary C code and may clobber a0/
* a1/d0 like any other call, so nothing computed before the "jsr" may be
* trusted afterwards.
         psect qatexit,0,0,0,0,0
atexit:
         move.l d0,d1
         tst.l d1
         beq ax_fail
         lea ax_count(pc),a0
         move.l (a0),d0
         cmpi.l #32,d0
         bge ax_fail
         lea ax_table(pc),a1
         lsl.l #2,d0
         move.l d1,0(a1,d0.l)
         addq.l #1,(a0)
         moveq #0,d0
         rts
ax_fail
         moveq #-1,d0
         rts

tc_run_atexit:
         lea ax_count(pc),a0
         move.l (a0),d0
         lea ax_index(pc),a0
         move.l d0,(a0)
         beq ax_done
ax_loop
         lea ax_index(pc),a0
         move.l (a0),d0
         subq.l #1,d0
         move.l d0,(a0)
         lea ax_table(pc),a1
         move.l 0(a1,d0.l),a1
         jsr (a1)
         lea ax_index(pc),a0
         tst.l (a0)
         bne ax_loop
ax_done
         rts

	align	4
ax_count:	dc.l	0
ax_index:	dc.l	0
ax_table:	ds.l	32
	ends
